.SS "int32_t schultz_window_options::maximized"
Nonzero opens the window maximized, filling the desktop's work area\&. The width and height above become the size the window returns to when it is restored\&. Ask schultz_window_width and schultz_window_height for the size it actually opened at\&.
.PP
Maximizing needs a window a desktop is allowed to resize, so this turns resizable on whatever it was set to\&. 
.PP
Definition at line \fB136\fP of file \fBschultz_window\&.h\fP\&.
.SS "const char* schultz_window_options::video_driver"
Which SDL video driver to ask for, such as 'offscreen' for a window nobody sees or 'kmsdrm' for a board with no desktop\&. NULL, which is what a zeroed struct holds, lets SDL choose, which is right on every machine that has a desktop running\&.
.PP
Read once, when the first window opens, because that is when SDL settles the choice\&. Setting it on a later window does nothing\&.
.PP
What the environment says still wins: SDL_VIDEO_DRIVER set by whoever is running the program overrides this\&. That is the right way round\&. A program says what it would like; a person running it on hardware nobody anticipated gets the last word\&. 
.PP
Definition at line \fB151\fP of file \fBschultz_window\&.h\fP\&.
.SS "int32_t schultz_window_options::resizable"
Nonzero lets the user drag the window's edges\&. On by default, because a window a desktop cannot resize is the unusual one\&.
.PP
Zero fixes the window at the size it opened at\&. That is what a kiosk or a fixed layout wants, and it is what a phone gets either way, since the window is the screen there\&. 
.PP
Definition at line \fB160\fP of file \fBschultz_window\&.h\fP\&.
.SS "int32_t schultz_window_options::allow_rotate"
Nonzero lets the screen turn with the device\&. On by default\&.
.PP
Only phones and tablets turn, so this does nothing on a desktop\&. It is a separate switch from \fCresizable\fP because the two are separate questions that one platform flag happens to answer: a kiosk wants a window nobody can drag the edges of and may still want it to turn, and a window that cannot turn is not thereby fixed in size\&.
.PP
The application still has the last word\&. An Android activity that declares \fCandroid:screenOrientation\fP, or an Info\&.plist naming one orientation, is not overruled from here: this can only narrow what those already allow\&. 
.PP
Definition at line \fB175\fP of file \fBschultz_window\&.h\fP\&.
.SS "uint32_t schultz_window_options::orientation"
Which way up the screen may be used: SCHULTZ_SCREEN_ANY, SCHULTZ_SCREEN_PORTRAIT or SCHULTZ_SCREEN_LANDSCAPE\&. Any by default\&.
.PP
Naming one of the two holds the screen that way whichever way the device is turned, which is what a kiosk display wants\&. With \fCallow_rotate\fP still on it turns only between the two ways up that match: a portrait screen turns end over end and never onto its side\&.
.PP
Phones and tablets only, for the same reason as above\&. A panel that is physically mounted the other way round is a different problem and this does not solve it; see docs/building\&.md\&. 
.PP
Definition at line \fB189\fP of file \fBschultz_window\&.h\fP\&.
.SS "uint32_t schultz_window_options::turn"
How far to turn the picture on its way to the panel: SCHULTZ_TURN_AUTO, _NONE, _90, _180 or _270\&. Auto by default\&.
.PP
This is the low level of the same idea as \fCorientation\fP, and it is what a fixed panel needs\&. Auto asks \fCorientation\fP instead: a portrait interface on a landscape panel turns a quarter, and anything already the right shape does not turn\&.
.PP
Naming a turn is the only way to say which quarter, and the only way to say upside down\&. A board whose display is fitted head down needs SCHULTZ_TURN_180, and no description of the shape wanted can imply it\&.
.PP
Ignored where the platform turns the screen itself, which is phones and tablets: there the device does the turning and this would turn it twice\&. 
.PP
Definition at line \fB207\fP of file \fBschultz_window\&.h\fP\&.
.SS "uint32_t schultz_window_options::threads"
How many worker threads the rasterizer may use\&. Zero rasterizes on the calling thread, which is reproducible; a small number is faster on a large window\&. 
.PP
Definition at line \fB213\fP of file \fBschultz_window\&.h\fP\&.
.SS "int32_t schultz_window_options::accessible"
Nonzero describes the window to whatever assistive technology is running, so a screen reader can read it\&. On by default, because a toolkit that has to be asked for accessibility is one that ships without it\&.
.PP
Starting it costs nothing when nothing is listening, and a reader may connect at any time afterwards\&. 
.PP
